diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage on it_lab3; its network stack and path are healthy now. On the host, eth0 is up with 192.168.1.5/24 and the correct default route via 192.168.1.1. From it_lab3, pings to the first hop 192.168.1.1 succeed with 0% loss (avg ~0.73 ms), and to a same-LAN peer 192.168.1.2 also succeed. End-to-end connectivity beyond the local LAN is fine: it_lab3 can reach office_pc (192.168.3.2) across the routed core with 0% loss and pc1 (1.0.0.5) likewise. ARP/neighbor entries are resolved for 192.168.1.1, .1.2, and .1.3; iptables/nftables show ACCEPT policies and no blocking rules; tc shows no queue/backlog. The NIC reports link detected and no RX/TX errors or drops. On router0, the LAN-IT interface (192.168.1.1) is up and routing tables are normal.

Mechanism: If the switch port or host NIC were “dead,” we would see link down, ARP failures, or persistent packet loss. Instead, ARP resolution and ICMP both work, and no errors or drops are counted, so there is no evidence of a failed port in the network.

Localization: Host it_lab3 eth0 and router0 eth0 are operating normally.

Root cause: No persistent infrastructure fault found; the earlier symptom was transient or local to the workstation and has self-cleared.

Corrective fix: None required now. If the symptom recurs, swap the patch lead, reseat the NIC, and monitor the switch-facing port for flaps; replace the cable/NIC if flapping is observed.
